  ga_desc: "Photos and videos from Tripack's trips in Madagascar: red tracks, lemurs, sifakas, zebu markets, southern beaches and sunsets.",
  ga_h1: 'On the road with Tripack', ga_lead: 'Pictures taken on the road, trip after trip: tracks, lemurs, villages and ocean.', ga_videos: 'Videos', ga_photos: 'Photos', ga_go: 'Travel with Tripack', close: 'Close',
  in_title: 'Practical info for travelling in Madagascar with a driver: season, roads, budget',
  in_desc: 'Preparing your trip to Madagascar: best season, road conditions, money, health, visa, tipping and what to pack. Answers from Tripack, a local driver.',
  in_h1: 'Practical info', in_lead: 'Everything you need to know before hitting the road with Tripack.',
  roads_h2: 'Roads in Madagascar',
  roads_p: "Distances are deceptive: in Madagascar you count in hours, not kilometres. On the paved RN7 the average speed is about 50 km/h because of potholes, villages and zebus. On the western tracks it's often 20 to 30 km/h. We never drive at night: it's a safety rule.",
  pack_h2: 'What to pack',
  pack_list: ['A fleece and a windbreaker for the Highlands (Antsirabe, Fianarantsoa)', 'Light clothes, a hat and sunscreen for the South and West', 'Good closed walking shoes (Isalo, Ranomafana, Tsingy)', 'A head torch for night walks and power cuts', 'Mosquito repellent and a small first-aid kit', 'A scarf against the dust of the tracks'],
  tipping_h2: 'Tipping', tipping_p: 'Park guides, boatmen and hotel staff appreciate a tip, which is an important part of their income. Tripack can tell you what is customary along the way.',
  fady_h2: 'Respecting the fady', fady_p: "Fady are traditional taboos that vary from region to region: don't point at a tomb, don't bring pork to certain places, don't swim in certain lakes… Your driver will let you know — that's also what travelling with a local is about.",
  faq_h2: 'Frequently asked questions',
  faq: [
    ['What is the best season to travel in Madagascar?', 'The dry season, from April to November, is the most pleasant on the RN7 and the only possible time for the West coast and the Tsingy de Bemaraha. From January to March it is the rainy and cyclone season: some tracks become impassable.'],
    ['Can you rent a car without a driver in Madagascar?', 'It is possible but not recommended — most rental companies offer cars with a driver anyway. Damaged roads, sand tracks, ferries, few signs: a local driver is a guarantee of safety and saves you a lot of time.'],
    ['How long does the RN7 take?', 'The road from Tana to Tulear is about 950 km. You can drive it in 2 or 3 days, but to visit Antsirabe, Ranomafana, Isalo and the other stops, allow 9 to 12 days.'],
    ['Is the Tsingy de Bemaraha open all year round?', 'No. The park is only accessible in the dry season, usually from May to mid-November (dates to be checked each year). Access is by 4x4 from Morondava, with a ferry crossing at Belo-sur-Tsiribihina.'],
    ['Can you drive from Tana to Nosy Be?', 'Yes: about 1,100 km along the RN4 and RN6, in 5 to 6 days with stops (Ankarafantsika, Mahajanga, Ambanja). The car stops at the port of Ankify, from where a boat reaches Nosy Be in about 45 minutes. Avoid the rainy season (January to March).'],
    ['Is the Fianarantsoa–Manakara train running?', 'The FCE line links Fianarantsoa to Manakara on the East coast, but its timetable is irregular. Tripack checks locally and can drop you at a station and pick you up on arrival.'],
    ["What does the driver's price include?", "Usually: the vehicle, the driver, fuel, and the driver's accommodation and meals. Park fees, local guides and your own accommodation are extra. Ask Tripack for a detailed quote."],
    ['What currency should I use?', 'The currency is the ariary (MGA). ATMs are found in towns (Tana, Antsirabe, Fianarantsoa, Tulear, Morondava) but become rare on the West coast: bring cash before heading onto the tracks. Euros are easy to change.'],
    ['Do I need a visa for Madagascar?', 'A tourist visa is required for most nationalities. It can be obtained online or on arrival. Rules and fees change: check the official website before you leave.'],
    ['What vaccinations and health precautions?', 'See your doctor or a travel clinic before leaving. Malaria is present, especially on the coasts: ask for advice on preventive treatment. Drink bottled water only.'],
  ],
  other_q: 'Another question? Write to us',
  ct_title: 'Private driver quote in Madagascar – Contact Tripack',
  ct_desc: 'Request a free quote from Tripack, driver-guide in Madagascar: RN7, West coast, Tsingy, South-East, Nosy Be. Quick reply on WhatsApp.',
  ct_h1: 'Request a quote', ct_lead: 'Tell me when you are coming, how many you are and what you want to see. I will reply quickly with a proposal.',
  prices_h2: 'Prices', prices_p: "Every trip is tailor-made: the price depends on the length of the trip, the route (paved road or 4x4 tracks) and the number of travellers. It usually includes the vehicle, the driver, fuel, and the driver's accommodation and meals. Park fees, local guides and your accommodation are extra. Send your plans and you will receive a detailed quote, with no obligation.",
